---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets"
import CaseStudy from "@/layouts/case-study.astro"
import { getProject } from "@/data/projects"
import homepageImg from "@/assets/projects/creative-printing/creative-printing.png"

const project = getProject("creative-printing-order-flow")!

const pictureFormats: ("avif" | "webp")[] = ["avif", "webp"]
const pictureWidths = [640, 960, 1280]
const pictureSizes = "(min-width: 768px) 960px, 100vw"
---

<CaseStudy
  project={project}
  title="Creative Printing Order Flow"
  seoTitle="Creative Printing Order Flow · UX Redesign"
  seoDescription="Redesigning Creative Printing’s homepage order path with responsive icon-grid navigation, clearer information architecture, and direct routes into service intake."
>
  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    Creative Printing’s homepage needed to help customers start the right kind
    of order: copy and print, business cards, envelopes, wide format, signage,
    multimedia conversions, website work, or general service questions. A
    business card order, a sign request, and a website refresh do not need the
    same questions or the same next step.
  </p>

  <h2>The Grid</h2>
  <p>
    I restructured the homepage around the “Order Now” moment. The main job
    categories sit in a large icon grid, labeled in the words customers use
    rather than the shop’s internal service names, and each tile routes to the
    relevant service page or intake form. Cleaning up the navigation instead
    would still have hidden the categories one level down.
  </p>

  <div class="order-flow" aria-label="Creative Printing order flow">
    <div class="order-flow__node">
      <span>Homepage</span>
      <strong>Order Now</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="order-flow__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="order-flow__node order-flow__node--wide">
      <span>Category grid</span>
      <strong>Recognize the job type</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="order-flow__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="order-flow__node">
      <span>Next step</span>
      <strong>Service page or intake form</strong>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure class="case-figure">
    <Picture
      src={homepageImg}
      alt="Full Creative Printing homepage: logo and phone number, main navigation, an illustrated storefront hero with an Order Now button, the eight-tile order grid, and a location map."
      formats={pictureFormats}
      widths={pictureWidths}
      sizes={pictureSizes}
    />
    <figcaption>
      The full page. The hero carries a single “Order Now” button, and the grid
      directly below it repeats that choice as eight labeled entry points, so
      the order path is the first thing on the page that is not navigation.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Outcome</h2>
  <p>
    The shop owner reviewed the grid and intake paths before launch. Seven years
    later the category grid still anchors the live site.
  </p>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  .order-flow {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 1.75rem 0;
  }

  .order-flow__node {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 0.375rem;
    padding: 1rem;
    background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--background) 96%, var(--muted));
  }

  .order-flow__node span {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
    color: var(--muted-foreground);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .order-flow__node strong {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .order-flow__connector {
    width: 1px;
    height: 1.25rem;
    margin-left: 1.25rem;
    background: var(--border);
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .order-flow {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 2rem minmax(0, 1.2fr) 2rem minmax(
          0,
          1fr
        );
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0;
    }

    .order-flow__connector {
      width: 100%;
      height: 1px;
      margin-left: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
